// part DOES and asks the student to say it — the parts and their doc pointers
// are the level-3 bargain. Every reference points BACKWARDS: 9.1, 9.2 and 7.1.
const DECOMPOSE: DecomposeSpec = {
  big: 'Your biggest level sheet leaves the project as 654,657 bytes and is 16 MiB of pixels before anything touches it. "Make it smaller" is not one problem: it is working out what the pixels cost raw, knowing which of the two families a file is allowed to use, and being able to say what each one threw away.',
  frame: 'concept',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Work out what the pixels cost before any compression',
      does: 'Width × height × 4 bytes, because one pixel is a `Color32` — four bytes, one per channel. That number is what every ratio in this lesson is measured against.',
      doc: 'the four-bytes-per-colour rule from 9.2, and the size table at the top of this lesson\'s Concept Lab',
      href: '9.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Say which family a format belongs to',
      does: 'Lossless returns the original bits exactly, every time. Lossy returns something close enough and never gives the original back, because the compressed file IS the file now.',
      doc: 'the lossless-vs-lossy row of the MC trap catalog, and the PNG-vs-JPEG table in this lesson\'s Concept Lab',
      href: '../exam/mc-strategy.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Find the repetition a lossless format can exploit',
      does: 'A run of identical values becomes one `(count, value)` pair, so sixteen symbols can cost four. Where there is no repetition — a checkerboard, pure noise — the same scheme makes the file bigger instead.',
      doc: 'the worked run-length example in this lesson\'s Concept Lab',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide which family each file in the project is allowed to use',
      does: 'A sprite the player stares at, a scene file, a number that has to come back exact: lossless. A photograph, a music track, a texture bound for the GPU: lossy is a bargain somebody took on purpose.',
      doc: 'Step 4 of the Build Lab below, and the sprite-slicing pipeline you ran in 7.1',
      href: '7.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Measure the file you already have before you re-compress it',
      does: 'A PNG has already been squeezed, so zipping it changes nothing or makes it larger. A `.unity` scene is text that has never been squeezed at all, and it shrinks thirty-six times over.',
      doc: 'the two measurements in Step 3 of the Build Lab below',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'The split is the whole lesson. "Compress this file" is one job you can only do by opening a tool; these five are five things you can check, argue and measure. The first is arithmetic you already own, the third and fifth are measurements you run on your own project, and the second and fourth are what the exam actually asks — every compression question on the paper is a question about which family a situation demands, and what it is allowed to lose.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you settle the part with a calculator and a file listing? "Work out what the pixels cost" gives you a number; "decide what this file may lose" gives you a reason. A part that needs a compressor installed before you can even think about it is not a part of this problem.',
  turns: [
    'A 64×64 sprite is exactly 16 KiB of pixels raw and 825 bytes as a PNG on disk, and compressing that PNG again gives you 858 bytes — bigger than the file you started with. Explain both numbers in one sentence each, and say which of them would change if the sprite were a photograph instead of flat pixel art.',
    'Your project ships the same 589,301-byte picture twice, and nothing in the project points at the copy in `OriginalFiles/`. Deleting either copy breaks no reference; deleting the wrong one costs a future re-slice. Which do you delete, and what does your answer assume about the next person to open the project?',
  ],
